WEBVTT

00:00:01.310 --> 00:00:06.460
I'm Dr. Sunny Aslam, and this is a guide
to using an ambient scribe well.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:10.620
An ambient scribe listens to the visit and
drafts your note.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:13.720
DAX is the best known one.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:15.320
The tool is fast.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.100
Your job is to make sure the note is true.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.240
Step one, ask.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.060
Ask your patient, say,

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:26.460
I use a tool that listens to our visit and
helps me draft my note.

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.680
This way I can focus on you instead of the
computer.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.100
It does record our conversation.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:33.420
You can say no.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.600
It won't affect your care.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:36.860
Is that okay?

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.240
If the patient hesitates, I turn it off.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:41.960
Step two, record.

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:46.160
Start with the patient's name and the
reason for the visit out loud.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:50.860
Then have a normal visit.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.500
Forget the phone is there.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:54.460
Step three, verify.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.000
This is the part that matters.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.560
Read the draft against the transcript.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:01.100
Check the negations.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:04.060
A dropped "no" flips the sentence's
meaning.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:05.760
Check left and right.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.600
Check every dose and drug name.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:10.780
Watch for invented findings.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:13.320
Things in the note that nobody said.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.040
And check who said what.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:19.020
The scribe tries to tell speakers apart,
but often gets it wrong.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:23.440
Mostly when a third person or more are in
the visit like family or staff.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.900
If the sentence matters and you can't
confirm it, delete it.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:31.080
In psychiatry, a couple extra checks.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.720
Risk statements must be exact.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:36.480
A summary can soften a threat or a plan.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:38.060
And quotes must be real.

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:41.260
If it is in quotation marks, the patient
said those words.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:43.480
Step four, make it yours.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:45.640
Tell the scribe what you want.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.480
Short notes or full ones.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:50.080
A tight HPI, your format.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.480
Set your style once and save it.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:56.920
A rule I use for sensitive details, such
as trauma and abuse included.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:00.900
If a detail does not change care, I leave
it out.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:04.260
Chart the fact plainly.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.660
Context rules, though.

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:08.980
Some patients need a fuller note.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.980
The level of detail is your call.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.420
Lastly, know when to keep it off.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:18.660
If the patient's unsure, if a third person
has not agreed to be recorded,

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:22.520
if a recording would change what the
patient tells you, use your judgment.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.280
A scribe works for you, not the other way
around.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:30.000
A full checklist is on my website with
links to Microsoft's official guides.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:31.100
Link is below.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:32.160
Thank you.
